THE MIT LICENCE ABOVE COVERS THE CODE IN THIS REPOSITORY. IT DOES NOT COVER
THE BUNDLED DATA.

This repository ships derived data files — river networks, terrain-derived
attributes, gauge records, project registers, geological units, glacier
outlines and others — built from third-party sources. Each of those retains
its own upstream licence, and some carry terms more restrictive than MIT,
including at least one non-commercial clause.

Those terms bind you regardless of anything written above. See LICENSES.md for
the per-file audit: which upstream dataset each file derives from, its licence,
whether redistribution and commercial use are permitted, and the attribution
each source requires.

If LICENSES.md is not yet present in your copy, treat every file under
`public/` and `src/data/` as separately licensed and check the build script in
`pipeline/` that produced it before redistributing or using it commercially.

---

MERIT HYDRO: THIS PROJECT ELECTS ODbL 1.0.

MERIT Hydro v1.0.1 (Yamazaki et al. 2019) is dual-licensed by its authors — CC
BY-NC 4.0 or ODbL 1.0, at the licensee's choice — per the vendor's data
policy at https://global-hydrodynamics.github.io/MERIT_Hydro/. HydroRecon
elects ODbL 1.0, and accepts what that election carries: derived data based on
MERIT Hydro is published under ODbL 1.0.

That applies to `public/nepal-upa.dat`, the one file in this repository built
from MERIT Hydro — one log-quantised per-vertex catchment area, parallel to the
river vertex block. It is offered as a Derived Database under ODbL 1.0.

Cite: Yamazaki, D., Ikeshima, D., Sosa, J., Bates, P. D., Allen, G. H., &
Pavelsky, T. M. (2019). MERIT Hydro: a high-resolution global hydrography map
based on latest topography datasets. Water Resources Research, 55, 5053-5073.
https://doi.org/10.1029/2019WR024873

The original MERIT Hydro GeoTIFFs are NOT in this repository and will not be.
That is not a licence term under either arm — the authors separately request
that the data not be redistributed whole and in its original format without
written permission, and that request is honoured.
